  "readMinutes": 7,
  "sections": [
    {
      "headingEn": "Two leading professional bodies",
      "bodyEn": "If you want a professional accounting designation in Australia or New Zealand, two bodies dominate the landscape: CPA Australia and Chartered Accountants Australia and New Zealand (CA ANZ). Both are well-established professional accountancy organisations whose designations are widely recognised by employers.\n\nNeither is universally \"better\" — they offer parallel routes to a respected qualification, with differences in program structure, exams and the experience you must complete. The right choice depends on your career goals, your employer's preference and how each pathway fits your study plans. Confirm current program details on each body's official website."
    },
    {
      "headingEn": "The CPA Australia pathway",
      "bodyEn": "CPA Australia awards the Certified Practising Accountant (CPA) designation. The pathway generally combines accredited academic study, a series of professional-level subjects and exams, and a period of relevant practical experience completed under guidance.\n\nCPA Australia has a large international presence, which can suit students who expect to work across the Asia-Pacific region. The exact subjects, entry requirements and experience component are set by CPA Australia and can be updated, so check its official membership pathway pages for the current structure before you plan your route."
    },
    {
      "headingEn": "The CA ANZ pathway",
      "bodyEn": "Chartered Accountants Australia and New Zealand awards the Chartered Accountant (CA) designation across both countries. The CA Program typically combines accredited academic study with professional modules and exams and a structured period of mentored practical experience.\n\nCA ANZ is the chartered body spanning Australia and New Zealand, which can be attractive if you intend to work in either country or move between them. As with CPA, the precise entry requirements, modules and experience rules are set by CA ANZ and may change — verify them on the official CA ANZ website."
    },
    {
      "headingEn": "How to choose",
      "bodyEn": "Because both designations are respected, base your decision on fit rather than prestige.",
      "bullets": [
        "Employer preference: some firms favour one designation; ask employers in your target area.",
        "Geography: consider where you plan to work — across Asia-Pacific, or specifically Australia and New Zealand.",
        "Program structure: compare the subjects, exam format and practical-experience requirements on each body's official site.",
        "Your degree: check that your degree is accredited by the body you choose (see the related accredited-degrees guide).",
        "Long-term plans: think about audit, tax, advisory or corporate roles and which pathway aligns best."
      ]
    },
    {
      "headingEn": "Accreditation comes first",
      "bodyEn": "Whichever body you choose, you usually need an accredited degree to enter the professional program. CPA Australia and CA ANZ each accredit university programs and publish recognised qualifications, and a single accredited accounting major often satisfies the academic entry requirements for both.\n\nBefore enrolling in a degree, confirm on the professional body's website (and the university's course page) that the program is accredited for your intended pathway. This protects your route to membership and avoids needing extra bridging study later."
    }
  ],
