  "titleEn": "Adjusting to Life in a New European Country: Culture Shock and Daily Norms",
  "descriptionEn": "How to adapt to European daily norms — punctuality, quiet hours, recycling, Sunday closing and bureaucracy etiquette.",
  "readMinutes": 7,
  "sections": [
    {
      "headingEn": "Culture shock is normal",
      "bodyEn": "Moving to a new country often brings a settling-in period sometimes called culture shock: excitement, then a dip as small daily differences pile up, then gradual adjustment. It is a common, temporary experience, not a sign that you chose the wrong place.\n\nEurope is not one culture. Norms around directness, formality, social distance and daily routines differ sharply between, say, the Netherlands, Italy, Germany and Sweden. Expect to learn the specific habits of your host country and city."
    },
    {
      "headingEn": "Punctuality and directness",
      "bodyEn": "In many Northern and Central European countries, being on time is taken seriously for classes, appointments and social plans. Communication can also be more direct than you are used to, which is usually meant practically rather than rudely.\n\nOther regions are more relaxed about timing and more indirect in style. Observe how local students and staff behave, and when in doubt for official appointments, arrive on time."
    },
    {
      "headingEn": "Quiet hours and housing rules",
      "bodyEn": "Many countries and buildings observe quiet hours (for example in the evening, at night, and often on Sundays) when you should avoid loud noise. Shared housing usually has rules on cleaning, guests and noise. Recycling and waste separation can be strict and detailed, so check your municipality's official guidance so you sort correctly.",
      "bullets": [
        "Keep noise down during local quiet hours",
        "Learn your building's rules on guests and shared spaces",
        "Sort waste correctly — recycling is often mandatory and detailed",
        "Be considerate in shared kitchens and laundry areas"
      ]
    },
    {
      "headingEn": "Sunday and shop closing",
      "bodyEn": "In several European countries, many shops, including supermarkets, close on Sundays and public holidays, and some have shorter weekday hours than you may expect. Pharmacies and essential services usually have rota arrangements.\n\nPlan grocery and errand shopping around these hours, especially before a weekend or holiday. Opening rules vary by country and region, so confirm locally."
    },
    {
      "headingEn": "Bureaucracy etiquette",
      "bodyEn": "European administration tends to value paperwork, appointments and deadlines. Many tasks — registration, residence permits, bank accounts — require booked appointments and original documents, and processes can feel formal. Always follow the official, current instructions from the relevant authority or your university's international office, and keep records of every submission and appointment.",
      "bullets": [
        "Book appointments early; slots can be limited",
        "Bring originals plus copies of key documents",
        "Keep a folder of letters and confirmations",
        "Note deadlines and respond to official letters promptly"
      ]
    },
    {
      "headingEn": "Looking after yourself",
      "bodyEn": "Adjusting takes time. Building a routine, joining a society or sport, staying in touch with home and using your university's official support services all help.\n\nIf you feel persistently low or overwhelmed, reach out to your university's student support or counselling services. This is general guidance, not medical advice."
    }
  ],
  "faqs": [
    {
      "questionEn": "Is culture shock normal when moving to Europe?",
      "answerEn": "Yes. A settling-in dip after the initial excitement is common and usually temporary. Building routines, joining student groups and using your university's official support services all help you adjust."
    },
    {
      "questionEn": "Why are shops closed on Sundays in some European countries?",
      "answerEn": "Several European countries restrict Sunday and public-holiday trading, so many shops including supermarkets close. Hours vary by country and region — plan shopping ahead and confirm local opening times."
    },
    {
      "questionEn": "How strict is recycling in Europe?",
      "answerEn": "Waste separation is often mandatory and detailed, with separate streams for paper, glass, packaging and organic waste. Rules differ by municipality, so check your city's official waste guidance to sort correctly."
    },
    {
      "questionEn": "Why does everything need an appointment?",
      "answerEn": "Many European administrative tasks — registration, residence permits, banking — run on booked appointments and original documents. Book early, bring originals and copies, and follow the authority's official current instructions."
    }
  ],
