      "bodyEn": "Germany has two main types of degree-awarding institution. A Universität (university) is research-oriented and offers the full range of subjects, including those leading to a doctorate. A Fachhochschule (FH) — often branded today as a Hochschule für Angewandte Wissenschaften (HAW), University of Applied Sciences — is more practice- and profession-oriented, with applied teaching, industry projects and frequently a mandatory internship semester.\n\nBoth award recognised bachelor's and master's degrees within the same European (Bologna/ECTS) framework. Neither is inherently 'better' — they suit different goals. The right choice depends on whether you want an applied, career-focused programme or a research-heavy academic one."
