      "bodyEn": "It helps to separate two things that students often blur together. The first is the English- or French-language proof a university asks for to admit you — that is an academic admission requirement set by the institution. The second is the Canadian study permit, an immigration document handled by Immigration, Refugees and Citizenship Canada (IRCC).\n\nMeeting a university's language requirement does not by itself satisfy any immigration requirement, and the reverse is also true. Which language evidence IRCC recognises for any given study-permit pathway is decided by IRCC, not by the university. This is general information, not immigration advice — always verify the current rules on the official Government of Canada source."
