      "headingEn": "Integrated master's vs BSc plus separate MSc",
      "bodyEn": "The alternative is a three-year BSc followed by a separately applied-for one-year taught MSc (or a research master's). Both routes can reach a master's level, but they differ in structure, funding and flexibility.\n\nAn integrated master's is a single application and a continuous programme, which can feel smoother and is often designed as a coherent four-year arc. A BSc-plus-MSc lets you change subject, university or even country between the two stages, and to decide on a master's only once you know your results and interests. Neither is universally \"better\" — the right choice depends on how certain you are about your subject, your funding, and whether you may want to switch institutions.",
