  "slug": "engineering-licensure-fe-and-pe-exams-explained",
  "category": "exam-prep",
  "region": "usa",
  "titleEn": "Engineering Licensure in the US: The FE and PE Exams Explained",
  "descriptionEn": "The US path to becoming a licensed Professional Engineer — the FE exam, work experience, the PE exam, and which disciplines rely on it.",
  "readMinutes": 8,
  "sections": [
    {
      "headingEn": "What it means to be a licensed Professional Engineer",
      "bodyEn": "In the United States, a Professional Engineer (PE) is someone licensed by a state board to take legal responsibility for engineering work — for example, signing and sealing engineering designs and reports. Not every engineer is licensed, but in certain fields and roles a PE license is essential.\n\nLicensure is granted at the state level, so the precise requirements are set by each state's licensing board, not by a single national authority. The national exams, however, are developed and administered by NCEES, which standardizes the FE and PE exams used across states.\n\nThe path generally has four stages: earn a qualifying degree, pass the Fundamentals of Engineering (FE) exam, gain qualifying work experience, and pass the Principles and Practice of Engineering (PE) exam. Always confirm the exact steps with the official board where you intend to be licensed."
    },
    {
      "headingEn": "Step 1: The FE exam",
      "bodyEn": "The Fundamentals of Engineering (FE) exam is generally the first step toward becoming a PE. It is a broad, computer-based exam covering engineering fundamentals, and many students take it near the end of their undergraduate studies or shortly after graduating.\n\nMost state boards expect FE candidates to be graduating from or have graduated from an engineering program — commonly an ABET-accredited one. Passing the FE typically lets you be recognized as an Engineer Intern or Engineer-in-Training, a stepping stone toward full licensure.\n\nExam content, formats, scheduling, fees and eligibility are set by NCEES and the state boards and can change. Verify the current FE details on the official NCEES site rather than older summaries."
    },
    {
      "headingEn": "Step 2: Work experience, then the PE exam",
      "bodyEn": "After passing the FE, candidates generally need to gain a period of qualifying engineering experience, often under the supervision of a licensed PE. The required amount and what counts are defined by each state board, so check your board's current rule.\n\nWith the experience requirement met, you take the Principles and Practice of Engineering (PE) exam, which tests deeper competency in a specific discipline. Passing the PE, along with meeting all your board's requirements, leads to full PE licensure.\n\nLicenses must also be maintained over time, often with continuing-education requirements that vary by state.",
      "bullets": [
        "Earn a qualifying engineering degree (commonly ABET-accredited).",
        "Pass the FE exam (often near graduation).",
        "Gain qualifying engineering experience defined by your state board.",
        "Pass the discipline-specific PE exam.",
        "Meet all state-board requirements and maintain the license over time."
      ]
    },
    {
      "headingEn": "Which disciplines rely on licensure most",
      "bodyEn": "Licensure matters more in some disciplines than others. It is most central in civil and structural engineering, where engineers routinely sign and seal designs for buildings, bridges and public infrastructure, and is also common in mechanical and electrical engineering for roles involving public safety, consulting or signing off on designs.\n\nIn fields like software, aerospace and many research or product-development roles, formal PE licensure is less common, because the work may not legally require a sealed engineering document. Many engineers in these areas never pursue a PE.\n\nLicensure is a career tool, not a measure of skill. If you may work in public-facing infrastructure, consulting, or roles that require sealing designs, plan early — choose an ABET-accredited degree and take the FE near graduation. If your field rarely requires it, you can still pursue it later. Verify all current requirements with the official state board and NCEES."
    }
  ],
  "faqs": [
    {
      "questionEn": "What is the difference between the FE and PE exams?",
      "answerEn": "The FE (Fundamentals of Engineering) is usually the first step, taken around graduation, covering engineering fundamentals. The PE (Principles and Practice of Engineering) comes later, after qualifying work experience, and tests deeper competency in a specific discipline."
    },
    {
      "questionEn": "Do all engineers need a PE license?",
      "answerEn": "No. Licensure is essential in fields like civil and structural engineering and common in some mechanical and electrical roles, but many engineers in software, aerospace, research and product roles never need it. It depends on whether your work requires sealing engineering documents."
    },
    {
      "questionEn": "Do I need an ABET-accredited degree to get licensed?",
      "answerEn": "Most state boards expect a degree from an ABET-accredited engineering program, though some allow alternative paths with additional requirements. Requirements are set by each state board and change over time — verify with your board."
    },
    {
      "questionEn": "How much work experience do I need before the PE exam?",
      "answerEn": "A period of qualifying engineering experience is generally required, often under a licensed PE, but the exact amount and what counts are defined by each state's licensing board. Confirm the current rule with the board where you plan to be licensed."
    },
    {
      "questionEn": "Can international graduates become licensed PEs in the US?",
      "answerEn": "It can be possible, but requirements for non-ABET degrees and international credentials vary by state board and may involve extra evaluation. This is general information, not legal or immigration advice — verify with the specific state board and NCEES."
    }
  ],
