      "headingEn": "BA vs BS in psychology — how the tracks differ",
      "bodyEn": "Many universities offer a Bachelor of Arts (B.A.) and a Bachelor of Science (B.S.) in psychology. The difference is not about prestige; it is about emphasis. A B.A. usually leaves more room for humanities, language, or breadth electives, while a B.S. typically requires more natural-science, mathematics, statistics, and laboratory coursework.\n\nIf you are aiming toward research, neuroscience, or quantitative graduate work, a more science-and-lab-heavy track can be useful preparation. If your interests are broader, the B.A. may fit better. Neither track is universally superior — compare the exact requirements on each department's official page and pick what matches your goals.",
