      "headingEn": "Understand What CLAT Tests",
      "bodyEn": "The Common Law Admission Test (CLAT) is conducted by the Consortium of National Law Universities for admission to the five-year integrated law programmes at participating National Law Universities, and its score is also accepted by several other law institutions. The undergraduate test is designed as an aptitude and comprehension test rather than a test of prior legal knowledge.\n\nThe paper is built around five sections: English Language, Current Affairs including General Knowledge, Legal Reasoning, Logical Reasoning, and Quantitative Techniques. Most sections are passage-based, so strong reading comprehension underpins your whole score. There is negative marking for wrong answers, so accuracy matters.\n\nExact question counts, marks, duration, and the marking scheme can change between cycles. Always confirm the current exam pattern on the official Consortium of NLUs website before you build your plan.",
