  "readMinutes": 7,
  "sections": [
    {
      "headingEn": "What the AIBE Is",
      "bodyEn": "The All India Bar Examination (AIBE) is a national certification examination conducted by the Bar Council of India (BCI). It assesses a law graduate's basic readiness to practise as an advocate in India.\n\nClearing the AIBE leads to the Certificate of Practice, which is required to practise law in Indian courts. The exam is therefore a key step that follows your law degree, rather than an admission test for studying law.\n\nBecause the format, syllabus, and procedural details are set by the BCI and can change, treat the official Bar Council of India website as the authoritative source and verify all current specifics there."
    },
    {
      "headingEn": "Where the AIBE Fits in Becoming an Advocate",
      "bodyEn": "Becoming a practising advocate in India generally follows a sequence: complete a recognised LLB, enrol with a State Bar Council, and clear the All India Bar Examination to obtain the Certificate of Practice.\n\nIn other words, enrolment with a State Bar Council and the AIBE are separate, complementary steps. The law degree alone does not grant the right to practise; the AIBE is the certification stage that follows.\n\nThe precise sequence, timelines, and any conditions attached to enrolment and the exam are governed by the BCI and the relevant State Bar Council. Confirm the current process on their official sources before acting."
    },
    {
      "headingEn": "Who Can Appear and What It Covers",
      "bodyEn": "The AIBE is intended for law graduates moving toward practice, in line with Bar Council of India rules on enrolment and certification. Eligibility is tied to holding a recognised law degree and the enrolment framework set by the BCI.\n\nThe examination is designed to test basic knowledge across core areas of law that a practising advocate is expected to be familiar with. The specific subjects, weightage, and question format are published officially.\n\nEligibility conditions, attempt rules, languages offered, and the detailed syllabus can change between editions. Always verify the current eligibility and coverage on the official Bar Council of India website rather than relying on older summaries."
    },
    {
      "headingEn": "How to Approach AIBE Preparation",
      "bodyEn": "Because the AIBE focuses on core, practice-oriented legal knowledge, preparation usually centres on revising fundamental subjects from your LLB and becoming comfortable with the relevant bare provisions of law.\n\nFamiliarity with bare Acts and core concepts is generally valuable, and many candidates revise systematically across the announced syllabus. Practising with the official format helps you manage time in the actual exam.\n\nNo guide or programme can guarantee that you will clear the exam. The most reliable approach is to study the official syllabus, understand the current rules on permitted materials and format, and revise the core subjects steadily. Confirm all such details on the official BCI source."
    },
    {
      "headingEn": "After the AIBE",
      "bodyEn": "Once you clear the AIBE, the Bar Council of India issues the Certificate of Practice, which enables you to practise law in Indian courts in accordance with the applicable rules.\n\nFrom there, advocates build their careers across litigation, advisory work, and many other paths, all under the professional framework set by the Bar Councils. The AIBE is the certification gateway, not the end of professional requirements.\n\nKeep track of any continuing obligations and procedural updates through the Bar Council of India and your State Bar Council. As rules can change, verify current requirements on their official sites before relying on them."
    }
  ],
