      "id": "vs-si",
      "headingEn": "Constable vs Sub-Inspector — which route is which",
      "bodyEn": "Candidates often weigh the constable route against the Sub-Inspector (SI) route, so it helps to be clear on how they differ. The constable is the entry-level rank, typically open at the school-leaving level (Class 10 or 12), with a written test pitched at that level plus physical tests. The Sub-Inspector is a higher, supervisory officer rank that usually requires a graduate degree and a more demanding written stage — some states even run a two-tier Preliminary and Main written exam for SI, and some add an interview/viva.\n\nIn short, if you have completed school, the constable route is the usual entry point; if you are a graduate aiming for a supervisory officer role, the Sub-Inspector route is the one to look at. Both are state-run with their own notifications, so confirm eligibility for the specific rank you want — see the companion Sub-Inspector guide for that route.",
