      "headingEn": "Sub-Inspector vs Constable — which route is which",
      "bodyEn": "The Sub-Inspector and constable routes are frequently confused, but they sit at different levels of the state police. The constable is the entry-level rank, typically open at the school-leaving level (Class 10 or 12), with a school-level written test plus physical tests. The Sub-Inspector is a higher, supervisory officer rank that leads constables — it usually requires a graduate degree, a tougher written stage (sometimes a two-tier Preliminary and Main), and in some states an interview/viva.\n\nSo the choice comes down to your qualification and target level: a school-leaver typically enters through the constable route, while a graduate aiming for a supervisory officer role targets the SI route. Some states also route SI recruitment through the State PSC rather than a police board. Confirm the exact rank's eligibility and stages in the official notification — see the companion constable guide for that route.",
