      "headingEn": "What an air traffic controller does",
      "id": "role",
      "bodyEn": "Air traffic controllers (ATCs) manage the safe, orderly and efficient movement of aircraft — on the ground, during take-off and landing, and while cruising through controlled airspace. Working from control towers and area control centres, they issue instructions to pilots, maintain safe separation between aircraft, and coordinate with other controllers as flights pass from one sector to the next.\n\nIn India, air traffic services at most airports are provided by the Airports Authority of India (AAI). The work is high-responsibility and procedure-driven, demanding sharp situational awareness, clear spoken English and calm decision-making under pressure. It is a career distinct from becoming a pilot or cabin crew — the ATC never leaves the ground but is central to every flight's safety."
